  #line-at(72.024pt, 82.92pt, "second order BDF time-stepping scheme is employed, with the maximum Courant number ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 103.58pt, "limited to ~0.3. Linear finite elements were adopted for both the velocity components and the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 124.34pt, "pressure field owing to their computational efficiency, and their ability to suppress spurious ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 144.98pt, "numerical oscillations, thereby improving the overall stability of the simulations. The ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 165.74pt, "computational domain was discretized using a non-uniform mesh, composed of triangular ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 186.38pt, "elements. The mesh was most-densely refined near the pool’s free surface and the droplet-air ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 207.14pt, "interface, where the element size was at least ~100 times smaller than the droplet radius. A ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 227.78pt, "relative tolerance of 0.001 was applied to all dependent variables to ensure smooth and stable ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 248.54pt, "convergence of the numerical solution. Furthermore, isotropic diffusion stabilization with ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 269.21pt, "tuning parameter of 0.25 was employed to suppress any numerical inconsistencies. For the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 289.97pt, "last phase of grid independence study, the number of mesh elements was varied from ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 310.61pt, "~366196 to ~774722. The resulting changes in the radial and axial velocity components were ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.37pt, "noted to be less than 0.5%.  ")
